<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Astyages</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/585-bce">585</a>–<a href="/years/550-bce">550 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Astyages was the last king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/median-empire">Median Empire</a> in the sixth century BCE. According to tradition he was the grandfather of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a>, who revolted against him. Cyrus's victory over Astyages around 550 BCE ended the Median Empire and marked the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/astyages.jpg" alt="Astyages" width="210" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Designed by Maximilien de Haese, Woven by Jac. van der Borght (1771-1775) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Astyages_submitting_to_Cyrus.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Astyages was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyaxares">Cyaxares</a> and succeeded him in 585 BCE, after the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kizilirmak">Halys</a> ended a five-year war between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydians">Lydians</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medes">Medes</a>. He inherited a large empire, which he ruled in alliance with his brothers-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus of Lydia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>. His wife <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aryenis">Aryenis</a>, the sister of Croesus, had been married to him to seal the treaty between the two empires. The ancient sources report almost nothing about his reign. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> portrays him as cruel and despotic, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesias">Ctesias</a> gives a favourable account, so both are regarded as biased.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> tells how Astyages dreamed that his daughter Mandane would bear a son who would destroy his empire. He married her to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-i">Cambyses I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anshan">Anshan</a>, whom he took to be no threat, and after a second dream sent his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harpagus">Harpagus</a> to kill the child Cyrus. Harpagus gave the infant to a shepherd, and when Cyrus was found alive at ten, Astyages spared him on the advice of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magi">Magi</a>. Modern scholarship generally rejects Herodotus's claim that Cyrus was Astyages' grandson.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harpagus">Harpagus</a> took revenge for a banquet at which Astyages is said to have fed him his own son, and he urged Cyrus to rebel in 553 BCE. After three years of fighting, Astyages' troops mutinied during the battle of Pasargadae. The contemporary Chronicle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a> names the mutiny as the cause of his overthrow. Cyrus then captured the capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecbatana">Ecbatana</a>. Ancient sources agree that Astyages was treated with clemency, though they differ. Herodotus says he stayed at Cyrus's court for the rest of his life, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesias">Ctesias</a> says he became governor of a region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthia">Parthia</a> and was murdered by a political opponent. The circumstances of his death are not known.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Astyages” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At that point the Persian kingdom was a minor vassal of the Median Empire under <b>Astyages</b>; within thirty years it would become the largest empire the world had yet seen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Lydian kings married into neighbouring dynasties: Alyattes gave his daughter Aryenis to <b>Astyages</b>, son of the Median king, to seal the peace of 585 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-lydia">Kingdom of Lydia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Power was reinforced through ritual, elite networks and marriage alliances, among them the marriage of <b>Astyages</b> to the daughter of the Lydian king Alyattes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/median-empire">Median Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 550 BCE Cyrus II, king of Anshan, defeated the Median king <b>Astyages</b> and took Ecbatana, then went on to found the Achaemenid Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-anshan">Kingdom of Anshan</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecbatana">Ecbatana</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in the Parthia Satrapy. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.81,48.52&amp;year=-568" aria-label="Ecbatana on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5781 904 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Astyages"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6231 1174h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6231 1174h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.81,48.52&amp;year=-568">What was here in 568 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
